From 6353857171a449075126216f11d24b65ca450ccd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dancingdeveloper
 <306930456+thedancingdeveloper@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 04:05:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] perf(ci): cache the image builds and stop rebuilding the
 pod toolchain
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The `dev` build of 2026-08-18 took 34 minutes. Measured from its log:

  1344s  stage-4 — the dev-pod toolchain (apt, rustup, 5x cargo install,
         sway, GStreamer, JDK, Gradle, Android SDK, Flutter)
   259s  exporting a tarball into the local Docker daemon for `load: true`
   243s  exporting and pushing the second, near-identical build
   215s  cargo build --release
   178s  core-build (uv sync)
   174s  web-build
    75s  generating the second build's SBOM

The Rust compile everyone suspected was 10% of it. The other 90% was work
that either did not depend on this repository's source or was done twice.

Root cause: no build cache anywhere. All four `build-push-action` calls ran
without `cache-from`/`cache-to`, and `docker/setup-buildx-action` provisions
a fresh, randomly-named builder per job and tears it down after — so every
run logged `booting buildkit` and not one `CACHED` layer, and the
`--mount=type=cache,target=/app/engine/target` in `engine/Dockerfile` was
empty every time. 458 crates compiled from nothing on every push.

Five changes:

1. Registry layer cache (`vogt-buildcache`) on every image build. A registry
   and not a cache mount or a `keep-state` builder, because `node-b` is a
   fleet: a job lands on whichever worker is free, so builder-local state is
   cold on most runs by construction. Needs nothing deployed to hold it.

2. The dev-pod toolchain moves to `engine/Dockerfile.pod`, published by
   `pod-base.yml` as `vogt-pod-base:{lean,full}-<hash>-<week>` and consumed
   by digest as `POD_BASE_IMAGE`. Content-hashed so an edit republishes
   immediately, week-stamped so the `latest`-at-build-time installs (rclone,
   sccache, gh, step, Gradle, selkies) cannot silently freeze. Flutter is now
   the difference between the two variants rather than a build arg.

3. cargo-chef splits the Rust build into a dependency layer keyed only on
   Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock and a workspace build on top, with `web/dist` copied
   in after it. Two bugs fixed at once: cache mounts could not cross a runner,
   and `web/dist` being a rust-embed compile-time input meant a stylesheet
   change recompiled all 458 dependencies. Cache mounts removed rather than
   kept, since a target dir that is a cache mount is not in the layer.

4. Each image is built once, not twice. Pushed under a `candidate-<sha>` tag
   outside both release streams, smoke-tested at its digest, then promoted
   with `docker buildx imagetools create`. What gets signed is now the same
   bytes that ran rather than a second build asserted to be equivalent.

5. The runner-side `pnpm build` is deleted from both workflows. Its output
   never reached an image: `engine/Dockerfile.dockerignore` lists `web/dist`,
   so it was excluded from the build context, and stage 1 builds the bundle
   the image actually embeds. The test that guarded the ordering was green
   over that inert step; it now reads `engine/Dockerfile`, which is what makes
   the guarantee true.

The agent CLIs deliberately did not move into the base and are bucketed by
day via `AI_CLIENTS_EPOCH`: they are documented as latest-at-build-time, and
both a weekly base image and a warm layer cache would have frozen them
without anybody deciding to (#23's lesson, kept).

Nothing here is deployed and no new estate infrastructure is introduced. The
cache and the pod base live in GHCR; production still moves only when the
digest pinned in `indexarr/ops` changes and Komodo runs `DeployStack`.

